{"id": "language-p00134", "blocks": [{"id": "language-p00134", "text": "6. Vocal Encoding and Canonical Transliteration", "style": "HEADING_1", "sourceParagraph": 134}, {"id": "language-p00135", "text": "Formal carrier syllables", "style": "HEADING_2", "sourceParagraph": 135}, {"id": "language-p00136", "text": "0: ma", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 136}, {"id": "language-p00137", "text": "1: ki", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 137}, {"id": "language-p00138", "text": "2: nu", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 138}, {"id": "language-p00139", "text": "The full sung carrier is the family onset followed by one carrier syllable per trit. LIF:012 is ni-ma-ki-nu; MOV:012 is gi-ma-ki-nu.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 139}, {"id": "language-p00140", "text": "Local compact-root algorithm", "style": "HEADING_2", "sourceParagraph": 140}, {"id": "language-p00141", "text": "Ordinary compact speech compresses the three address trits into one consonant-vowel-consonant root.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 141}, {"id": "language-p00142", "text": "First trit → initial consonant: 0=n, 1=k, 2=m", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 142}, {"id": "language-p00143", "text": "Second trit → vowel: 0=a, 1=o, 2=i", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 143}, {"id": "language-p00144", "text": "Third trit → final consonant: 0=m, 1=k, 2=n", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 144}, {"id": "language-p00145", "text": "000: nam", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 145}, {"id": "language-p00146", "text": "012: non", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 146}, {"id": "language-p00147", "text": "111: kok", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 147}, {"id": "language-p00148", "text": "222: min", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 148}, {"id": "language-p00149", "text": "The same local compact root occurs at the same address in every family. Family identity is supplied by the harmonic opening and, in human writing, by the family onset.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 149}, {"id": "language-p00150", "text": "Canonical human transliteration: family onset + middle dot + local compact root", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 150}, {"id": "language-p00151", "text": "LIF:000: ni·nam", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 151}, {"id": "language-p00152", "text": "MOV:000: gi·nam", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 152}, {"id": "language-p00153", "text": "The local form nam remains a valid field and may be sufficient inside a clearly established harmonic context, but family-qualified transliteration is the canonical written form.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 153}], "outline": [{"id": "language-p00134", "title": "6. Vocal Encoding and Canonical Transliteration", "level": 1}, {"id": "language-p00135", "title": "Formal carrier syllables", "level": 2}, {"id": "language-p00140", "title": "Local compact-root algorithm", "level": 2}]}